WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:39.780
<v SPEAKER_1>We were right in the middle of those sand dunes when the whole place lit up like daylight. We both looked up, and there was this really bright light focused on us from about 600 feet up. It wasn't perfectly round, it was oblique, and you could see panel lines in it, everything, and it was slowly rotating. The crickets all stopped, the frogs, you couldn't hear anything. We were just kind of staring at that light for maybe six or eight seconds, and the light winked out. The medic, when I walked in the door, he said, what do you do, fall asleep on the beach? Because you're bright red. I just like bright red skin. He said, I obviously had a radiation burn.

00:00:49.931 --> 00:01:35.011
<v SPEAKER_2>Hi, everyone, and welcome back to That UFO Podcast. As always, my name is Andy, and my guest today is one I am incredibly excited for. I'll just say this now, I have a small list of names that since I started the podcast six years ago, I've wanted to speak to. And now and again, a name pops up, and this is one of those names I've wanted to speak to for some time. So my guest today is a former US. Army Intelligence Officer and the first remote viewer recruited into the US. Government's Psychic Espionage Program, later known as Project Stargate. Over nearly two decades, he participated in hundreds of operational sessions for US intelligence agencies and was awarded the Legion of Merit for his work. Welcome to the podcast, Joel McMoneagle.

00:01:35.131 --> 00:01:37.711
<v SPEAKER_1>I'm glad to be here, Andy.

00:01:37.711 --> 00:02:22.791
<v SPEAKER_2>Very good to have you on, sir. I'm glad you're fighting fit. You're feeling well. A lot of folks have been asking for this one. Originally, we were going to speak back in November, I think it was, 2025. You had a little issue to deal with at that time, but you have very kindly kept in touch with me. We've got it sorted out. Here we are now in February 2026. And Joe, you have lived what must be one of the most fascinating lives I've ever read about, listened to or watched through various interviews over the years. But what I want to do here is give listeners a flavor of what your entire life has been like. But can you take us back to your very early years? And I want to know, what was your upbringing like as a child? And were there any experiences that maybe in hindsight you feel were particularly relevant?

00:02:23.091 --> 00:05:38.191
<v SPEAKER_1>It all was particularly relevant. I grew up in a slum in Miami, down in Miami, Florida. And the area I was in, which was on about northwest Second Avenue and 79th Street, was mostly refugees, Cuban refugees, refugees from Papadocs Island, Chicanos, blacks. I was a token white kid in the neighborhood and there was about six gangs. And unfortunately I had four younger sisters. And so I had to take care of my sisters and keep them out of trouble. And so I grew up needing to know what the neighborhood was going to do before I could do anything. And so I got to be very observant, what I call situationally aware. And I would never agree to join a gang. But as I was growing up, I had an incident when I was about 15. I was with my mother downtown walking on a park across from the courthouse. It's right on the water. And it was a pretty hot day. And so I went over and got a drink out of a water fountain. And my mother had a meltdown because it said colored only. And so I got immediately taken to the doctor. The doctor read me to riot at. And I said, you know, this is crazy. You know, this is out. This is, it's really offensive. And I kept arguing against it. And the more I argued against it, the worse I got fed back information. And so I made a decision. I was going to get the hell out of Miami. First chance I got. So right after my 18th birthday, when I graduated from high school, I actually had two scholarships to universities. I skipped them both and went straight in the Army. And the Army, people say, the Army, why couldn't you have gone in the Coast Guard or something calmer? And the only reason I went in the Army is I talked to everybody. I went to the recruiter for Marines, Navy, Air Force, and even the Coast Guard. But I kept going back to the Army because that was the only person that wasn't lying to me. He kept throwing me out of his office. He kept saying, go somewhere else. All you're going to learn to do here is launch bullets and catch bullets. And you're too smart for that. But I kept going back there, so that's why I enlisted. And I think my test scores kind of escorted me into the intelligence area. They recruited me right after my AIT for a weapon. They called me in and told me that my weapon was obsolete, that they were taking it out of the Army. They weren't really. They were trying to push me out the door to look for work, which I did. And I met a guy with a beard and he recruited me. So that's how I wound up in intelligence.

00:05:39.411 --> 00:06:03.891
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah, and most of this interview is going to circle around the remote viewing aspect of your life, because it has played such a major part of your life. I often talk about remote viewing on this podcast. As much as the title is That UFO Podcast, you had a UFO experience before the Stargate program and the Bahamas. Can you just take us back to there? What happened and did it change how you saw reality?

00:06:06.911 --> 00:08:18.371
<v SPEAKER_1>Because I was in intelligence, I went to three more schools, and I graduated first in the class in two of them and second in the class in one. That was when they were block allocating everybody to Vietnam. But the top three students in each class got sent somewhere else. At the end of my three schools, I wound up in the Bahamas. The island was named Alutra. It was maybe half a mile wide at its widest point, and about 116 kilometers long. And they were going independent at the time from UK. And America offered some assistance. They said, no, we want to do it on our own. So we asked if they would allow us to put an air sea rescue unit in there, or a missile tracking unit for downrange from Cape Canaveral. And they said yes. So I was part of an eight man unit that was put on a Luthora, which was important because a Luthora was like the breadbasket for all the Bahamas. They grew all the food there. And so that's where I was for 18 months. I logged probably $3,800 diving. And it ended up rescuing some people off a couple of yachts. So it was in 1966. And that's where the UFO event happened. It happened one on a weekend when I and my partner, we worked in pairs, I and my partner, Steve, we had two or three days off, we were going to go fishing. So that night, we actually went to, we had an outdoor theater where the down range engineers and people from like William Bendix, RCA Victor, that kind of thing, they could watch these first run movies from the United States. It was outside because the weather was beautiful there all the time. So we went to the movies, double feature. It was the first James Bond movie came out. It was either Dr. No or From Russia Above. I can't remember.

00:08:18.371 --> 00:08:19.271
<v SPEAKER_2>Dr. No.

00:08:19.271 --> 00:15:30.351
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah. We like sitting outside because you could raise your hand and yell nurse and a young girl in a white outfit would come down and take a drink order. So you get pretty obliterated watching a movie. And after the movie, we went to the bar. We didn't have anything to do for two days. So we stayed in the bar till about 1 in the morning and then decided to go down to our quarters, which was closer to the ocean. And on the paved road, if you walk down on the paved road to the quarters, it was about three quarters of a mile. And it would take some time, but you could take a shortcut behind the bar. You could go over the top of the sand dunes. He had a big hill of sand dunes where the big cisterns were, where they captured rainwater. And you could cut through there and save half the time. So since we were going to go fishing at 7 in the morning, we took the shortcut. We were right in the middle of those sand dunes when the whole place lit up like daylight. I mean, it was like high noon. And of course, when everything lights up like that, you look for the source of the light. And so we both looked up, and there was this really bright light focused on us. From about 600 feet up, it's like a cone of light. And above it was this monstrous vehicle of some kind. It wasn't perfectly round, it was oblique. And you could see panel lines in it, everything, and it was slowly rotating. And when we were in the light, we couldn't hear anything. It was like being in a bell jar. The crickets all stopped, the frogs, you couldn't hear anything. And we were just kind of staring at that light for maybe six or eight seconds. And the light went out. And just about the time it came into focus, it vanished. It went away so fast. All I can tell you is we had no idea what direction it went. And in the years after, I've been thinking about it a lot, and it's quite possible that it might have just folded out of reality. I don't know. But the one thing I remember is that, you know, when you're a little tipsy and you look up, you got to hold on to something or you're going to fall down. And I was holding on to this small, young palm tree growing out of the sand with my right hand. When I looked up and the light went out and I looked down, the palm tree was on my left. It was only like eight seconds. And so I don't know what happened in that eight seconds. But the next morning, we went back to the bar and we had another six pack and discussed the whole thing and very quietly between us. And we made a decision we weren't going to tell anybody about it because the problem was we figured it. Well, first off, nobody would believe us. Secondly, they think we were crazy and we'd probably get shipped off to Vietnam. And we like being in the Bahamas. So we didn't tell anybody. We got down to the quarters. We went down on walked on the paved road this time. Got to our quarters and went to sleep. The next morning, I got up about 645 and I didn't feel well. I thought I had a really bad hangover. So I decided we were going to go fishing later. So I walked down to my buddy's quarters, Robert Roberts, and he wasn't there. His door was standing open. So I asked the man across from him, I said, where's Steve? And he said, oh, he went out of here at four in the morning in an ambulance. They took him to the airstrip and flew him to Florida today, to a hospital in Florida. I said, really? What was wrong? And he says, I have no idea. So I was feeling pretty bad too. And I was thinking, well, maybe he had something and maybe I should get checked out. So they had a small naval unit there that were medics. And so I reported on sick call there. And the medic, when I walked in the door, he said, what do you do? Fall asleep on the beach? Sleep on the beach all day? I said, no. Why are you saying that? And he said, because you're bright red. I had this like bright red skin. And so he said, fill out this form. And I started to fill out the form. And when he saw what I was putting on there, he freaked out. He took it away, tore it up, put it in a burn bag. He said, don't ever write that again. Just say, bad sunburn. So I put bad sunburn. And he gave me these pills to take for the next two weeks. He said, I obviously had a radiation burn. So it turns out that's what happened to Steve. He was in the hospital for two weeks and then came back. When he came back, it was interesting because I asked him when we were privately together, I said, Steve, what, do you remember what happened? He said, oh, yeah, I remember it perfectly. I said, okay, can you recall it for me? And he said, yeah, we saw this red truck. It was on the side of the road and we stopped and it had a problem with the radiator. And I was taking the cap off the radiator and it exploded in my face. And I said, no, no, no, that's not what I'm talking about. And so he said, well, that's what happened. And I said, okay, let's presume that that's what you believe. What happened before we saw the red truck? He couldn't remember. So what happened after the red truck? Couldn't remember. I said, Steve, there's not a red truck on this whole island. So where's this coming from? But to this day, he believes that. Now, now what's interesting about the whole thing as well, he was wearing a, we wore civilian clothes there. He was wearing a Bohemian shirt, you know, one that doesn't tuck in. It just has a straight hem around the bottom. And on the front, it had two stripes with a couple of palm trees sewn into the cloth. It was a light green. And to this day, when he opens his shirt, you can see the residuals of the palm trees and the two stripes on both sides. So, he got embossed with the palm trees. And I always kidded him about, where did that come from? He says he didn't remember. But to this day, he has no memory of the event. And I don't understand that.

00:15:30.351 --> 00:15:37.971
<v SPEAKER_2>Do you feel that event had any bearing on what was to come in your life in terms of the remote viewing capability?

00:15:37.971 --> 00:16:59.591
<v SPEAKER_1>No, I really don't. When I eventually did go overseas after the Bahamas, I went straight to Vietnam. And after Vietnam, I did some time in other countries. I spent nearly, well, almost 14 straight years overseas during an active intelligence mission. And eventually, they brought me back to the headquarters. But in all that time, that was just an event that happened in the Bahamas, and I've never forgotten it. I mean, it was really impressive. I have no idea what the vehicle was. It was real, I can tell you that, because you could actually see how it was constructed underneath. You could see the panel lines and the way it was put together. And it looked like reflective metal. And over time, I've considered that it probably did not fly away, probably just folded out of reality in some way. In that eight seconds, we both got burned pretty good. I don't think I got burned as badly as him because I was a diver. And I spent all the hours in the sun anyway and had some protection.

00:16:59.591 --> 00:17:07.411
<v SPEAKER_2>Did you ever in later years ask anyone to remote view the situation for you to find out more?

00:17:07.411 --> 00:18:00.651
<v SPEAKER_1>No. It's possible that they might have done that in the remote viewing unit, but it wasn't to my knowledge. They never told me. And when I was in the unit, the remote viewing unit, I was in it the rest of the time I was in the military, which is about seven years. And then when I retired, I went into the science side. I worked for Stanford Research Institute. When they shut it down there, I went to Science Applications International Corporation, which continued the remote viewing research. And then when they shut the whole project down, I went to Laboratories for Fundamental Research in Palo Alto and continued remote viewing up till two years ago. So I've been a remote viewer for 48 years.

00:18:00.651 --> 00:18:22.271
<v SPEAKER_2>So let's talk about that then. It's fascinating that incident happened and like you say, there's no bedding. It wasn't like some folks claim an experience happens, whether it's an alien or UFO experience. As some folks report out of body and near-death experiences and it causes something to happen. But for you, completely separate, how does the remote viewing aspect of your life begin then?

00:18:24.851 --> 00:23:37.711
<v SPEAKER_1>The reason they actually brought me into the remote viewing unit is when I came back to the United States to the headquarters, I did everything I could not to go to the headquarters. I had been 14 years in the field doing a real mission and I didn't want to push papers. And so I tried to get out of it. In Department of the Army, when I talked to the people there, they said, now this is a special request from a three-star. You can't, it's the commander for all the security and intelligence in the Army. And we can't, we can't change it. So what I did is I asked them what the highest demand was for a language. I had maxed my DLAT language test. And so they said it was Chinese Mandarin. So I said, okay, I'd like to read it, write it and speak it. So they said, well, for that we can change. And so I went to Monterey for, I had orders for two years, immersed in Mandarin. And when I got there, I was only there about three hours and I got this phone call at the Orly Room. And I went down to the Orly Room and it was a guy on the phone and he said, do you know who I am? And I said, no, sir, I don't. He said, I'm the guy that expects you to be in his office at nine o'clock on Monday. This was a Saturday. And he said, you better be there or, and he said some other things and then hung up on me. And the Orly, I was using a regular phone and the Orly all the way across the room said, wow, that sounded like an order to me. You could actually hear the whole conversation. The guy was yelling so hard. It was the general and he was really pissed off that I had gone to Monterey and not to his headquarters. So when I got to the headquarters, I found out why. What they did is that he decided I should be a warrant officer. So I got commissioned as a warrant officer, a chief warrant officer, and put in charge of my MOS for the world. So I was there. Now, in working for the general, I was his advisor and he was in charge of all the security and intelligence for the army. So it was a pretty important job. And in the processes of doing that, I made some decisions which got me to brief him on some things. And I think it kind of prevented him from walking into some ambushes or walking into some technical problems. And so he considered me as being somewhat like a radar or malle on mash or something, you know, always a half step ahead of him. So when he came out and said, we're going to do this new project, they didn't call it remote viewing and they called, I can't remember what it was called, Gondola Wish, I think. When we're going to do this new project, he said, I believe it's kind of hinky. And what he meant was he didn't trust it. He didn't know anything about it and it bothered him. So he sent me to Stanford Research Institute to ask questions about it. He wanted me to be fully briefed on it so I could protect his backside. So I went there and when I was there, I was asking so many questions. The people there said, well, look, if you do a couple, you'll understand what it is. And I said, do a couple of what? They said remote viewing. So I did, I did, I think six remote viewings over a three-day period, where they would send somebody somewhere and I would have to describe where they were. I said, I don't understand what you're asking me for, because I don't know where they are. How am I going to tell you anything about it? They said, well, just say whatever pops in your head. So I did some drawings for each one. And then when it was independently judged, it turns out that I was the first person to get five first place matches and one near first place, it was a second place match. And that's a record that hasn't been beaten since. So I got back to Arlington at the headquarters and my desk was empty. So I went down to the general's office. I said, why is my desk empty? And he said, I've decided I want you to be in this project. I'm going to send you to Fort Meade. So I went to Fort Meade and I got recruited into the project as the first remote viewer. That was in October of 78. So I got 001. What's funny is the next guy they were going to recruit was going to get 002 and the major said, no, no, no, no, you can't do that because then you'll be telling everybody how many remote viewers we have. So he got 385.

00:23:37.711 --> 00:23:40.071
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah, I love that. That's that's very clever.

00:23:40.071 --> 00:23:46.231
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah, that's kind of funny. But anyway, so I became a remote viewer.

00:23:46.231 --> 00:23:59.331
<v SPEAKER_2>When you mentioned the four matches, am I right in saying that the first match, like you say, is like near perfect? That's spot on. Second is close. Third is in the ballpark. But four is no match at all. Is it something like that?

00:23:59.331 --> 00:24:37.751
<v SPEAKER_1>Right. It was you could get one of four different scores. You get first, second, third or fourth. Fourth is absolutely nothing like it. Third is a relatively very small part. Second is maybe a 50-50. But a first place match is almost 90 percent or better. There's no question if you laid 50 photographs out on a table, you could take the drawing I did and match it to that photograph. And no, that's where the person was. So five first place matches was unheard of.

00:24:37.751 --> 00:24:41.831
<v SPEAKER_2>What were you thinking when you heard that you had been so accurate?

00:24:41.831 --> 00:25:33.911
<v SPEAKER_1>Well, I actually got in an argument with Russell Targ. He was one of the researchers. And we were sitting in this window, this room for like 20 minutes and he said, well, are you going to write something or draw something? And I said, what am I supposed to do? Just invent something. And he said, just whatever pops in your head. I said, okay, so you want me to invent something. So I closed my eyes and what popped in my head, I drew. And I made some mistakes now. Like one of the first place matches was a restaurant next to a yacht club where they had sailboats. And in front was a big parking lot. Well, I drew the restaurant exactly like it was. Put in the parking lot, the front, put in the sailboats on the left. But the thing I left out is the restaurant had a boat on the roof.

00:25:33.911 --> 00:25:34.611
<v SPEAKER_2>Right.

00:25:34.911 --> 00:25:53.151
<v SPEAKER_1>I said, nobody in their right mind would put a boat on the roof. He said, well, we don't understand why you left that out. I said, because nobody would ever put a boat on the roof. We do you crazy. But I saw the boat on the roof. I just didn't put it on there.

00:25:54.871 --> 00:26:14.711
<v SPEAKER_2>What was your first meeting like with Russell Targ? And I'll just say, folks, if you've not heard of Russell Targ, I interviewed him last year and it was wonderful, much like yourself, Joe, to speak to Russell. It was incredible to speak to an incredible gentleman, much like yourself. But how put off Russell Targ? What was it like meeting them? Was the first time together or was that separate?

00:26:15.471 --> 00:30:48.971
<v SPEAKER_1>I met them both at the same time. Russell is a unique individual. You're absolutely right. He's legally blind, but he rides a small motor scooter to work. And we always knew when he was arriving at Stanford because the research center, because you could hear all the horns off the cars beeping at him. He just fell right in front of him. So, but he was a unique guy. He pretty much a Buddhist from a spiritual standpoint and was always interested in the more spiritual sides of it. While Hal put off, the man in charge of the lab was more involved in the science, the protocol, keeping the protocol pure. The protocol is interesting. You can't, if you want to do a remote viewing, the actual protocol is you have to be in a room with whoever's in there with you, can't know what the target is either. So everybody's totally blind. There's no hints. You just sit there with a pad of paper and a pen or a pencil, and you try to figure out what it is that's coming into your head about that location. So what I've learned over the years, I've been doing this for 48 years now, I like to think of it as a martial art of the mind. When you first start, you can hit some things. Over about two or three years, you can get fairly good at it and produce information of good enough quality for intelligence. But over 10 or 15 or 30 years, you can really produce some interesting work. Just before I retired, I did a test for one of the agencies. I can't name the agency, but what I did is I tracked a spy. They asked me if I could track a spy and I said, sure. I was called three times over a few months. Usually it was at an inappropriate time. They called me on the phone and say, where's the spy now? All I had to target that person with was the Social Security number. Social Security number doesn't tell you if it's male or female, and they could be any age 14 to 75. And nobody in the lab, because I had been working in the labs for about 33 years by then, nobody in our lab knew where the person was from or who it was. And yet the first three times I was called, I drew the picture of exactly where that person was standing. So over the years, your skill set gets better and better. People think that remote viewing is simple, it's easy. It's just like closing your eyes and seeing something. But you learn a lot about it over time, like closing your eyes is not a good idea, because when you close your eyes, you're cutting out all of your perceptive powers for smell, touch, feel, emotion, everything, except visual. When you close your eyes, you're looking to see something. It's not the same thing as seeing, it's perceiving. And so calling it remote viewing was a mistake. It makes everybody think you only close your eyes and see something. Now, if that's all you think about it, that's not remote viewing. Secondly, a lot of people who don't do well, they'll give them a hint. Like if they're looking for a missing person, they may show them a picture of the person. That's a violation of the protocol. Because when you see the person, you'll start making assumptions about them based on how they look, what their sex is, how old they are, those kinds of things. You don't want to be doing that. You want to be getting totally what's coming in psychically. And so, any violation of the protocol is not remote viewing. It may be psychic functioning, but it's not remote viewing.

00:30:49.211 --> 00:31:20.671
<v SPEAKER_2>So let me say this then, Joe. Because I think some of the things that people level at you, for example, when I spoke to some of my friends to say, I've got a remote viewer coming on, this is what remote viewing is, a lot of them, when I say, what would you ask them? Because I like to do that sometimes. Folks have no interest in these topics, just to see what they would say. And a lot of the times, they come back with things like, well, why don't they just remote view the lottery numbers? But that's to your point, you can't just go and see something that you know, or you're expecting, it's got to be that blind target.

00:31:21.771 --> 00:36:00.311
<v SPEAKER_1>Well, over the years, I've been tested on words as an example. In the case where I was doing the words, we were a half a mile apart in two different trailers. And the people that were inventing, you know, were randomly choosing the word, and they were using these big white cards, one by one foot cards with a black letter. And they were spelling the word out on a whiteboard. They would put these cards up on a whiteboard on a ledge. So the word was clearly visible. Well, I did seven words, and I got five of them. So they said, you must be seeing the word. And I said, no, I don't think I am. I think what I'm doing is something different. So let's do two more words. And this time, scramble the letters, which they did. They scrambled the letters and just put them up on the wall. And I still got the words. And I think the reason why is what you're getting is the sense of the meaning of the word, and then working backwards to fit it in to the number of letters they want to see. Like, a seven-letter word or five-letter word, that sort of thing. So, it doesn't work the way you make assumptions about it. So, you have to be very, very careful. And the second problem is every human being on the planet is different. There's no two people on this planet that are identical. We all think differently because from birth to whatever age you are, you're different socially, economically, educationally, intelligently, religiously, spiritually, all these different things, your culture even, impacts heavily on how you believe, what you believe, how you think. So, you can't say remote viewing can be done by anyone. That's true. But only with some training can you say, I'm now wearing like a white belt for remote viewing. To get to the brown belt, it's going to take years, five years maybe. It actually, in training someone for remote viewing who has a natural talent, it takes about two years for them to produce things of value. So, in the military, when we used it, that training process was there. When I first started, I had 24 stray failures. And the primary reason why is my ego got in the way. I thought I was a great psychic because of the good job I did at Stanford. No, it's the exact opposite. You got to get your ego out of it because ego is what destroys it. There's just so many things you have to learn. And I think people want it to be easy, though. They think, oh, you just draw a picture where the person is, so it must be something you see. No, it's perception. And you have to build that picture bit by bit. And it takes a long time to learn how to do that in your head. And everybody is affected differently by it. Everybody perceives things differently. I think one of the difficulties comes out of the fact that when we walk into a place and we look at it with our eyes, we're thinking we're in our head looking through eyes and we're seeing something. That's not true. What's happening is we're seeing a field of nothing but energy, photons and whatnot, and our rods and cones and our sensors are picking it up, sending it to an area in the back of our skull where we're deciding what all that means. And so we're never actually seeing what's there. We're actually observing the parts that we need to assume what it is. So you can take 10 strangers into a room that's empty and leave them in there for 30 seconds and bring them out and say what's the room used for and you'll get 10 different answers because people only see what they need to understand what the room is. You understand where I'm going with this?

00:36:00.311 --> 00:36:11.891
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah, absolutely. Does remote viewing then, you say ego plays a part of it and bias can play a part of the interference in what you're perceiving.

00:36:11.891 --> 00:36:13.311
<v SPEAKER_1>Yes.

00:36:13.311 --> 00:36:24.411
<v SPEAKER_2>I'll tie this back in then. Many folks would say things like remote viewing or whatever you want to call it, have been something we as a species have been able to do for many, many, many millennia.

00:36:24.411 --> 00:36:27.951
<v SPEAKER_1>Oh, yeah. Hundreds of thousands of years.

