<v SPEAKER_2>Over the time, though, I suppose in modern days, you know, your coffee, your cigarettes, your alcohol, the technology around you, the religions around you, Western culture, wherever you live, all of that, then do you feel that dampens those abilities and suppresses it?

00:36:45.971 --> 00:42:41.631
<v SPEAKER_1>Well, the problem is, if you go back far enough, humans, for probably 10,000 years, had no language. There was no, letters didn't exist to make a language. All we could do was grunt and point and make emotional sounds, maybe. So in some way, we were communicating, and the communication had to be affected, because if the men are hunting and the women are gathering, we're vulnerable. If you're out there hunting a dangerous animal, and there's only six men, and you're very tiny little tribe, you got to know what each person's doing exactly when they're doing it in order to survive. So what remote viewing is based on, I think, what it comes out of, is this mechanism that I would call a survival mechanism. It's like the way we thought to stay alive. And we could interact with one another, probably in a psychic way, to ensure that survival. Where we came into a problem is when we started trusting other small tribes. I think most of the tribes of people are probably less than 15 people. They were probably more related to one another than not. They maybe take mates from other tribes in order to keep the DNA pool healthy. But we didn't trust any other tribes. So when two tribes started coming together and sharing a cave, some issues came up. If you can read the intentions of someone just by looking at them, that's a dangerous thing because now you're sitting across the fire from a stranger and he's maybe he's oogling your woman or he's checking your spear out and you just come across the fire and kill him because he's threatening your means of survival. That can't work. So Mother Nature does what Mother Nature does. It makes changes or it puts in filters to prevent that from happening. And over time, as you're building language and you're building the capacity for writing and keeping records, that mistrust is filtered in some way to tamp it down. So there's more cooperation and over time, you now have ways of going somewhere and reading about something, studying about something, getting an answer out of Google now. So you don't need to be that ultra-sensitive person anymore. That's what we've progressed to. The problem is, it's a very basic nature that we have that has caused us to become survivors against a world where there were way more capable animals with teeth, huge fangs, size. I mean, when you stop and think about the fact that we're the ones covering the earth now, and the cave bears aren't, it tells you something. It's that intellectual part, or the psychic part, that I think was part of the survival mechanism. And where you see that is you see it in combat with soldiers, surviving soldiers. You see it in the police departments, sheriff's departments, border guards, that sort of thing, where they operate alone in many cases or operate after dark. In order to survive, they have a situational awareness that's very heightened in comparison to your average person. And you also see it in firemen. Firemen will go into a building to fight a fire from different angles with the hoses, but they all seem to know when to back out before the building collapses. How's that work? And I've even talked to surgeons who have the ability to know what to do on a crunch in the surgical suite, where they don't have time to react. They just make the right decision. So, you know, it's part of our natural ability. It's just that more people have used it since birth, where others haven't. So, if I had not grown up, I think, in that ghetto, where I was at risk all the time, I probably would not have developed the ability that I had to survive any of the army experiences. It's certainly not the remote viewing would have been terrible, because I would have never used it before. I wouldn't even think about it. So, I think there are some people that just have a propensity for going in that direction. And those are the people who become really good remote viewers. The idea that anybody can do it is true. But the idea that you don't have to work at it is not. I mean, you really got to work at it. I've been working at this, improving my skillset for over 45 years. And I've never stopped. I kept hammering away at it. If you stop, your progression starts to fall away. Because it has a lot to do with your belief structure. It has a lot to do with what you accept as truth and not truth. So, it's a complicated issue.

00:42:42.891 --> 00:42:55.071
<v SPEAKER_2>Let's take you back to the Army then. And you've got those initial days and weeks underway. You've had those successful tests. You think there's something to this. What then happens? What's the process from there?

00:42:56.591 --> 00:47:03.471
<v SPEAKER_1>I think one of the major milestones is coming to having to accept the fact that you actually can produce something very specific about an area, place, an event, a thing that you have absolutely no access to. And the reason why is because if the protocol is kept pure, which means you have no access to any information about the target, everyone you're interacting with has no access to that, and you produce something that, for instance, saves a life. When you go look back at it historically, and you know what you did to affect that, you go, wow, that's a gift. That's something I've never realized that I could do. And it improves you immediately by your having to acknowledge that you can do something you never thought possible. So you grow. You grow spiritually, you grow physically, you grow mentally, you grow in all different ways. And that progressive improvement in your, in your active, in your abilities has a profound effect on you. You just keep getting better. Where the problem comes in, it's where that protocol is not kept pure. And let's say you're working on a missing person, or somebody who's been kidnapped, for instance. And somebody says, well, they were taken from this city. Well, they just introduced the probability of where they might have gone from there. When in fact, they might have changed their whole modus operandi anyway. When General Dozier was kidnapped in Ravenna, Italy, he was taken by the Rote Brigada, the Red Brigade. Now, their modus operandi was to immediately cross into an iron block country and disappear. And so that was the reason you could never find them, and you'd have to pay a ransom or something to get the person back. In this particular case, they had a 12-hour head start, and there were nine countries they could have gotten to in 12 hours. But they didn't do that. They changed completely. They went across the new town area to the old town area. It's the same city. And the reason we knew that is because two remote viewers walked in and said that. They said, he's being held in a city named Padua. And Padua was the archaic name for the very city he was taken from. So that told him two things. He's in the old part of the city, and he's in the same city. And that changed the whole Montesapur and I. So that made the finding of him much easier. Now there were a lot of other stuff that came to play. The police got a hold of a cousin or an uncle or something of one of the Red Brigade members who knew when they showed him a dead-end road that he had an apartment in that building. So the police said, well, if they got him there, they might have taken him into the apartment. And so, you know, a lot of things started coming together. That's how remote viewing works. It doesn't come up with the actual answer, but it comes up with bits and pieces that glue other information together. It does things that allow the analysis to have sort of an open window on one side, where they can see things that make the other things more valuable that they already know.

00:47:03.731 --> 00:47:15.291
<v SPEAKER_2>For you, Joe, was it a transition period from the early days of the initial testing to going into more serious cases and finding individuals? What was that transition like?

00:47:15.291 --> 00:52:12.511
<v SPEAKER_1>Well, I can give you a primary example. I wrote a paper on it 35 years ago, and it's an important paper because where I got the information from had nothing to do with remote viewing. I started thinking about what makes remote viewing work and work better, and what I noticed was all the elements that make any business in the world profitable has to be in remote viewing. And the very first thing out of that is intention. You have to intend to build a company that's going to make money. If you don't have a clear intention for how to do that, it's never going to happen. If you don't have everybody on the same intention, it's very difficult to make it happen. All your employees have to have a shared intention with you in order for your business to do well. Well, in remote viewing, it's the same thing. So one of the reasons why it worked so effectively in the Army is almost without exception, every case we worked on, and we worked for 17 of 19 major intelligence agencies in America, every case we worked on was a life and death issue. Maybe it was somebody taken by force and being tortured to death for information. Well, if they knew that, everybody in the room had the intention of saving that person to include the viewer. And you could feel it when you walked in the room. Everybody wanted to get to the same thing. It's unbelievable the way that consolidates information collection. It's incredible. So we were able to do really exciting things with the remote viewing in the military because almost without exception, all of our tasks were life and death issues. It made all the difference in the world. You go out and start playing with remote viewing on where did your buddy go last night when he had his girlfriend with him. No, that's not going to work because half the people in the room trying to conceptualize that don't care. It's one of the reasons you do an experiment with remote viewing in any lab. You start off with the fact that the scientists in charge doesn't care whether it succeeds or fails because they get to publish. That's their whole job. Keep a neutral interest in publish whether it failed or succeeded. The only person in an experimental viewpoint about remote viewing is the remote viewer who wants to be successful. Everybody else is off somewhere else in their head with intention. But if you walk everybody into a room and the scientist in charge goes up to the whiteboard and says, we want to be successful here because if we can't, we will get no money for more research and everybody will have to go find work. You see how that brings everybody together to be successful. Okay, so you get everybody in the same sheet of music with intention. So the second thing you got to worry about is the details of it. When you build an operation, they reach a certain point. You have to understand the steps that are necessary and all the details about that. If you have everybody on the same sheet of music, everybody understands the details that need to succeed. Then your expectation for outcome becomes the third thing that you get to worry about. If everybody shares the same expectation, guess what? It works like gangbusters. Throw a frivet in any of that and it starts falling apart. It's the way business works, so why shouldn't remote viewing work that way? Or why shouldn't artistic endeavor work that way? Or inventions work that way? Everything works that way, which is why I generated the paper. It's not simply you bring somebody in and say, you show a little psychic ability, so give us a psychic answer for this thing that we have a problem with. And the people standing in front of you know all about the problem. And half the people don't care if you're successful or don't believe you ever will be. It's not going anywhere. You understand?

00:52:12.511 --> 00:52:13.131
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah.

00:52:13.411 --> 01:03:17.391
<v SPEAKER_1>And the reason I could say that is I've got 35 plus years in research. On the research side, I've got 45 years in the collection side, and I've got all the in-between with my own company, where I supported major businesses, individuals, sheriff's departments, police departments, missing people, missing kids. I've got a lot of experience in this, and I've been tweaking that skill set all along the way. I thought I was the most powerful psychic in the world when they told me I had such a good result at Stanford. So I didn't realize that ego impacts on it heavily. Ego is where you want to be in charge of everything. You want to be in control of everything. And in remote viewing, you can't be in control of any of it. It has to be that you have to take your mind that's in control, and you have to back away, and you have to let whatever happens, happens. You can't make any assumptions. You just take whatever comes in and provide it. You don't make any judgment calls. So I went through 24 failures, and I was about to the point of quitting. And I had a target where I didn't get anything, but I got a major headache. And when we did the feedback, it was a pretty poor target. I didn't get much on it. I just started getting a headache. And I said, the headache, I can't make it go away. There's something about this target. It's giving me a headache. So we went to get the feedback. They took me to the actual target. And what it was, was the Fort Meade fire station, where they had a whole row of these fire trucks. And what I recognized when I saw the fire trucks is I kept getting this color, lime green. And in a million years, I would never would have said a fire truck was lime green because they're all red, except on Fort Meade, they're lime green. And so we're parked in front of the fire station. And I said, well, I remember seeing the lime green, but I'm wondering where the headache came from. And that's when all the alarms are off and all the doors opened and all the sirens came on. And we had to drive away from there very quickly because you couldn't even hear from all that noise. And I said, that's probably where I got the headache. So I started paying attention to more things about my perceiving versus seeing. That's when I started figuring it out, that remote viewing is not really about seeing, it's about perceiving. And so I started paying attention to taste, smell, hearing, sight, emotions, you know, physical impact. All these different things. And I started getting better and better at it. So my, where I really, really came through, was in, well, was in a couple months of my beginning, I've been doing exercises mostly, practice for about six months, trying to get better. And I finally got a couple operational missions to work on. And one of them had to do with a building in Severodvinsk, Russia. It's north of the Arctic Circle. There's a harbor there and the harbor's almost always iced in. They keep two big icebreakers parked at the entry. But it's a, it's sort of a secret area. And they have a huge building there. I mean, it's a monster building that you could put a whole shopping center in and it wouldn't touch the walls of the ceiling. I mean, it's just huge. And it had all kinds of guards around it and fencing and security. And they had railroad tracks going in. So they were taking in carloads of materials that were covered in the cars were coming out empty. And this had been going on for a number of years. And every intelligence agency had taken a crack at it. And nobody could get anything. They couldn't buy a picture of the inside of this building. I think they were offering millions of dollars for just a photograph of the inside. Couldn't get it. So that became my primary target. I and another man who was one of the original six viewers that was recruited, his name was Hartley Trent. And Hartley in many ways was a better remote viewer than I'll ever be. But Hartley and I were the two that were working on that particular target. And we both said the same thing on the same day. What we said was they're building a different kind of submarine. And the reason we said submarine is because it was, it looked like one. Only the difference was they were taking two sub hulls and putting them together. And then wrapping it. And they were using new welding techniques, where they were using high intensity lasers to do the welding. And some of the steel was eight inches thick or thicker. And they had moved their launch tubes from the rear to the front, which was a totally different aspect. And anyway, over three or four psychic missions against this particular building, between the two of us, we came up with the idea that they were building this monster submarine that was the biggest submarine ever conceived. And they asked me, I remember they asked me one day, the monitor I was working with. Now, the monitor noticed nothing either. What we found is the monitor can be as psychic as the psychic that's working the target. The monitor will sometimes ask exactly the right question to get the response that solves the problem. We don't know how that works, but it does. And the monitor said, well, exactly how big is it? And I said, it's around 30 feet shy of the length of a Soviet aircraft carrier. And they said, oh, that's not possible. I said, but that's true. And there were some other things about it. They slanted the missile tubes so they could launch on the move, where before they had to stop the launch. That made them vulnerable, things like that. And there were a whole lot of things I can't talk about, but we painted this huge picture of this huge submarine. And we sent that to the National Security Council. Now, National Security Council, this target had been bumped up to the National Security Council. They're like the end of the road. They get the problems that no one else can solve. And they only work on one. And this was the target they had been working on. And they had nothing. And unfortunately, the man who was in charge of the analysis team at the National Security Council was Robert Gates. Robert Gates was the head Russian analyst. He ran the whole Russian floor of analysts in Langley at the time, the CIA. So he was on loan to the National Security Council. Well, he got our report. And when he saw our report, he sent it back. He refused to take it. He said, this is total fantasy. And the reason he said it was total fantasy is because the building is not connected to the harbor. There's no way to launch anything out of this building. So he said it was total fantasy. Well, the man who brought it back to me, my remote being back to me, was an admiral by the name of Stuart. I had known Admiral Stuart for a number of years. So he knew me. He brought the reports back to me. And on top was a paper that said total fantasy, RG. And I said, who is RG? And he said, Robert Gates. And he explained to me that he was a senior Russian analyst. And I said, well, no, take it back. This is true. And he said, well, he's not going to take it. And I said, take it back. And in telling me, he's got to pay attention to this. So he said, well, I'll take it back. And he picked it up. And I said, wait a minute. I put my own paper on top. What I said was, fantasy will be launched in 112 days, Jay. And that was kind of a comical way of answering his note. It peeved me. It made me angry. And I wanted to like let Robert Gates know whoever Robert Gates was. He's an idiot. So Admiral Stewart took it. But what's interesting is he knew me. He knew that when I'm serious about something, it should be paid attention to. So en route back to the National Security Council, what he did is he went to the reconnaissance people, and he ordered up a couple overheads to take pictures 114 days out. He figured if I was correct, that would be the appropriate time. So that's what he did. And 114 days later, when they got their pictures back, there was this monster submarine parked right next to a Russian aircraft carrier. And they were hiding, they put it next to the aircraft carrier to hide it from view at the entry of the harbor. And he said, unfortunately, you were wrong about the length. It's not 30 feet shy of the length of the Soviet aircraft carrier. It's 33 feet shy of the length of the aircraft carrier. So it embarrassed the hell out of Robert Gates. Now, Robert Gates eventually became the director of the CIA. While he was the director, he shut the remote-viewing program down. I'm not saying the reason why here. I'm just saying it's interesting.

01:03:17.391 --> 01:03:17.871
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah.

01:03:17.871 --> 01:05:18.111
<v SPEAKER_1>And then he eventually became the secretary of defense. I believe he's a political animal, and he's not a real military-tuned person. But what in fact happened is they opened the side of the building up, cut a channel from the building corner to the harbor, and launched it out of the end of the building. And nobody knew that would happen. But we knew there was a submarine, and it was a different kind of submarine. This submarine is so wide in the back. It's like, oh my God, it's like 35 meters across width-wise. It's huge. And it actually is two tubes married, which nobody in their right mind would build a submarine that way. But they did. And it was the first, it was the TK-089. It was a prototype for the Typhoon class submarine, which could park off of any country, 100 miles off of any country, and eradicate it in 18 minutes, because it carried 20 MIRV-5s, all independent, directable warheads. So, it's a huge threat. What they did, the Russians did, is they made eight more of them. And we never heard about the other eight. We never knew they built them. So, the fact that we had all the intel from the photographs on that one, gave us enough information to defend against it, I believe. I don't know for sure. I don't care. All I know is I tipped them off, and Hartley Trent tipped them off to the fact that that's what they were building. And no other intelligence agency could collect anything on it.

01:05:18.111 --> 01:05:24.171
<v SPEAKER_2>Did the results of your work in that case change how the intelligence community treat you going forward?

01:05:24.171 --> 01:07:19.151
<v SPEAKER_1>Oh, it changed it dramatically. Now, part of the people that became knowledgeable of that were very supportive. We had some senators, Senator John Glenn, who was an astronaut, Senator Cohen, who has been a personal friend. Some of the senators were very supportive. They didn't care if it was psychics, they didn't care. We were getting good information and they felt very supportive of us. But there were almost an equal number of people on the other side who, for religious reasons, didn't want to be participating. We were doing the work of the devil as far as they were concerned. I had to give demonstrations sometimes to the select subcommittee for intelligence because they were the ones who would approve the money year to year. And the approval of the money that kept us in business was based on successes and failures. So we had to have at least enough successes to keep funding coming in for the next year. That kept us alive for 23 years. But eventually, the people that didn't like using psychics because it was an affront to them, religiously or spiritually, gained enough power to shut it down. That's my belief, only my belief, because I had senior senators walk away from me in rooms and accuse me of doing the work of the devil. I had other senators say I was doing God's work to keep it up.

01:07:20.031 --> 01:07:35.971
<v SPEAKER_2>That sounds very familiar, I think, to folks listening or watching this, the UFO topic. In recent years, we've heard about how many folks in the halls of government and the military, at the highest levels of power, see this as a demonic phenomenon. Absolutely.

01:07:35.971 --> 01:11:14.411
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah. And it's not. It's a threat. The whole idea behind the Stargate program was to address a threat. We knew that the Soviets were using psychics. We didn't know if that was a threat or not. So the Gondola Wish program, which is what it was called at the beginning, it was designed to only last for three years. The first year, they would search out and find psychic people in the Army with clearances, bring them to the headquarters, train them, and then send them out in the world to target only our stuff. You know, like the White House, the Pentagon, places like that. DIA, NSA, CIA. Target those buildings. And then, after a year of collecting, using only psychic ability, they would bring us back in, take that material and turn it over to an independent agency who would evaluate its value. If it was valuable, they would know then that it was a threat and needed to be countered. If it was trash, they know that that's probably as good as what the Soviets doing, and it didn't need to be defended against. What happened is we got the response on the submarine. Then we found a Russian plane that went down over the Congo in Africa. It was carrying nukes. Everybody wanted to find that plane, terrorists, everybody. They looked for that plane for five years. Nobody could find it. We had three psychics that found it in four hours. They put guys on, they roped in off the helicopters and met natives on the ground carrying parts of that plane back to their trot, their area to patch holes in the roofs of their huts. So, we found the plane, and President Carter gave an interview to news people where he actually said he was holding a folder and it said Grill Flame on it, which is the new name for the remote community unit. And a reporter asked him, how did you find the plane after so long? And he said, we used our psychics. It was no longer Grill Flame, it became Center Lane then and we all get moved. You know, everything changed. And we had our name change like five or six times because everybody wanted to know about us and everybody that found out about us talked about us. And it was pro and con and arguments. And, you know, all we wanted to do was do the work because it worked. It was a threat. And we needed to determine how do you block a remote viewer? Well, in 23 years, I can tell you, you can't. It's impossible. Now, what you can do is you can do exactly what they do now for encryption. You can delay them knowing something by hiding something. And what is that? Well, if you got a new kind of nuclear weapon, what you do is you park it in a bunker surrounded by old nuclear weapons.

01:11:14.411 --> 01:11:14.991
<v SPEAKER_2>Right.

01:11:14.991 --> 01:12:21.951
<v SPEAKER_1>Okay? It's hidden in a mass. You hide it in a pile of the same thing. Somewhat similar thing. A good remote viewer, it would take an excellent remote viewer to find it. Eventually, they would, but by then, it may not be as important to defend that information as before. It's like encryption. You encrypt something. I will tell you, there's no encryption that can't be broken. It may take a half a year to do that. By then, the information is no longer of value. So, it's of value, but they decided, for some silly reason, I don't know what it was, that they were going to shut it down, which they did. And it was of extreme value, as far as I'm concerned. Now, the CIA says it never did anything of value for them. Well, I can tell you, I did over 200 missions for the CIA. And they kept coming back for 23 years. So, that don't, that, as they say in America, that dog don't hunt.

01:12:21.951 --> 01:12:50.571
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah, that's what blows my mind with this, pardon any, any kind of pun that might come across there, that this capability, whether people see it as a weapon, as an asset, as a mechanism for defense or attack, would be shut down by a superpower that it just seems it was so useful and wielded such incredible results. Do you believe it still is happening now in some way, shape or form?

01:12:50.571 --> 01:15:21.371
<v SPEAKER_1>No, I don't, and here's the reason why. There's, basically, there's 40 years of research behind remote viewing. That's the knowledge of how to make it work the best way possible. No one's ever come and gotten that information. And I can say that because we're sending it all to Rice University in Texas, in Houston, Texas, because they're the historians for this stuff. No one from any agency's ever come and asked us for help. And I participated for 30, 35 years in the research side. I was tested the whole time, 35 years, and I never failed a test. In fact, I surprised many people. When the wall came down and things changed in Russia and other places, Ed May, the man running the unit, the research side, he and I went to Russia twice and we met our counterparts. And I actually did experiments in their DACA outside of Moscow with their top psychic. And blew the minds of the Russian scientists who had never seen something happen like we did. And what we basically did was real simple. Two deprivation chambers, half a mile apart, and we were told to decide first who was the center and who was the receiver and then pass a message. And we could invent that message. And we had two digipads in our laps. We were both in deprivation chambers. And we both arrived at the same conclusion simultaneously. You don't need to know who the center and receiver is if both have the same message, right? Okay. So we put the message on the board. And when they brought us to the control room and turned on the computers, it was a butterfly. You could fold it out. They were outside of some very tiny differences. It was identical. They wouldn't even let us take a copy of that home with us. So are the Russians still working on it? No. We know the three-star admiral that was running the program. They shut theirs down too.

01:15:23.871 --> 01:15:58.231
<v SPEAKER_2>That blows my mind. I think that's crazy. In general, we're going to move on to another session that happened, I believe, after your time in the Stargate program and what not, and on to some other parts of this conversation. But overall, what it says to me, I've written down here, surfing, like you said, you can't control remote viewing in a sense, but the information is always of a similar quality. You just get better. And for me, it's like a wave where you can learn to ride the wave and you can learn to ride the wave better, but you can't control the wave. Is that fair?

01:15:58.371 --> 01:16:33.271
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah, that's right. Yeah, that's a fair statement. If there's information there of value, that someone's attempting to hide or cover or blend in with something else, it will uncover it. You can't make it go away. If it's done properly, like I said, if you're following the protocol exactly, it's possible to get information of value out of anything. You can't prevent it.

01:16:33.271 --> 01:16:52.571
<v SPEAKER_2>So one of the cases that so many people wrote to me asking me to ask you about is the Ancient Mars Session. And I think that's when people bring up remote viewing, it's one of the most disgust cases. I know you'll have spoken about this, Joe, many times, but if you don't mind just reframing it for folks who may be unfamiliar in what that originally was all about.

01:16:53.751 --> 01:21:08.951
<v SPEAKER_1>What happened towards the end of my career, I was the only viewer left. Everybody else was dead, mostly from complications from Agent Orange, cancers, all that sort of thing. I survived. Actually, I almost didn't. At the end of my career, when I retired, I had five heart attacks and open heart surgery. That's directly responsive to being under a huge amount of stress for seven years. The stress was beyond belief. Working for these agencies was just, it can't even explain it. But at the time, when I was getting close to retirement, I was the only viewer left for about 18 months. So I was doing all the remote viewing. And I was having some problems with that. What they did is they decided they wanted to know, since I was always having spontaneous out-of-bodies from my near-death experience, they wanted to know if I could learn to control that. So what they did is they hired Robert Monroe, the man who authored Journeys Out of the Body, the first human to talk about out-of-body events. They hired him to train me, to teach me how to control, how to generate and control my otherwise spontaneous out-of-bodies. So the way it worked, I would do remote viewing from Monday morning to Thursday evening. When I finished my last remote viewing task on Thursday night, I would drive out to the Monroe Institute where Robert Monroe lived. And get there about midnight, 11 o'clock. And I would get up in the morning around 7.30 and meet him in his lab. And he would work with me in trying to train me on how to generate the out-of-body and control it. And we worked together in his lab on Friday, Saturday and Sunday. And then 4 o'clock in the morning on Monday, I would drive back to Fort Meade and do remote viewing. I did that for 14 months. And every weekend, that's what I did. And so over that period of time, I eventually was able to do the capability. I could control an out-of-body, I can generate it, I can go somewhere and I can collect intelligence. Now what's interesting is in doing that, I think it scared people because DOD, the Department of Defense, said that's, I did a demonstration, by the way, I did an actual target and was able to produce the details. And as a result, they shut it down completely. They said, we don't want you doing this anymore. You have to stop. Okay, it scared them because some of the data I collected was scary. So, but while we're doing this, while I'm working in the lab with Bob Monroe on weekends, the DOD showed up one day. I think it was a Saturday. And I had been working all morning in the lab and in the lab when I'm working, I'm in a black box where I can't see anything. It's like a deprivation chamber. So I'm in there, and these people from DOD showed up and said, we have a target for Joe. And so Bob Monroe said, what is the target? And they said, we can't tell you. They gave him an envelope, which he folded over and put in his shirt pocket. And then they said, we need some answers about locations. And they had a list, I think, of seven GPS locations on a card. And so I was taking a nap because it was lunchtime and I was taking a nap to rest. Because doing this was making me pretty tired.

01:21:08.951 --> 01:21:09.511
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah.

01:21:09.511 --> 01:24:35.511
<v SPEAKER_1>And so Bob Monroe woke me up. He said, I have some of your friends here from DOD and they want to know some answers to locations. And I said, OK, give me the locations. So he read me the GPS locations. The very first GPS location I got had a pyramid. And I said, oh, this must be a new discovery somewhere. And he said, well, I don't know what it is, but we're interested in whatever you're seeing. And so I was producing a lot of data about this pyramid. And I said, it's really weird because this pyramid is huge. It's way bigger than the one in Giza. And it's built differently because the rooms inside are huge. They're large, open rooms. I don't know how they did that from a construction standpoint. But I was talking about that. And then they gave me a second target location. And I went there and said some things about multiple pyramids. And then there was a straight road that went through some kind of an impact crater. And I was talking about this stuff. And the whole time, I thought I was on Earth. Because I had never done anything on another planet. And GPS locations for me was Earth bound. So I was just reporting what I was seeing or perceiving. And I started picking up on a background history of why there were no people there now. And that there had been an event that was cosmic in size. And that they had built these pyramids as sort of a survival method, a way of staying alive until somebody could rescue them. Because it affected the climate in that area and everything else. And so things started not making any sense to me. I was pretty tired by the time we got to the 7th location. So I said, I'm sorry, I can't do the 7th location. I just can't hold it any longer. And so they said, that's fine. So I came out and I said, so where... Oh, I remember during the session, I said to Bob Monroe, I said, you know, the sun looks really weird. And he said, I don't care about the sun. Keep working on what you're seeing or perceiving at that location. So I kept doing that. So I came out of the booth, the deprivation chamber, and I said, so what's this target? And they said, well, we don't know. We just brought it in the envelope. And Bob Monroe has the envelope. So I said, let me see the envelope. So Bob took it out of his shirt and he handed it to me. Now, none of us knew what the target was until I opened that envelope. When I opened the envelope and pulled the paper out, it said Mars, one million BC. That really pissed me off because I don't like doing targets that I don't have ground truth for. I need ground truth because that's the only way I can perfect my skill set.

01:24:35.511 --> 01:24:36.831
<v SPEAKER_2>Like, verification.

01:24:36.831 --> 01:27:16.531
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah. I need to be able to verify at least part or piece of it. So I got really angry. And I said, give me the card. So they gave me the card with the GPS locations. I kept the envelope in the paper. And I said, I'm keeping all this. And they said, why? And I said, because the next time I'm on the West Coast, I'm going to go to Jet Propulsion Laboratory, where they store all the films. And I'm going to get film confirmation for this. And they said, they'll never give it to you. And I said, no, they're going to give it to me. I was really angry. So next time I went to the West Coast, to Stanford Research Institute, I took a day off and I went to the JPL. And I went to the counter where they sold all the negatives and pictures and everything that they take of space. Now, what most people don't know is every picture they take in space is done with tax money. So they can't classify those pictures. They have to release them. But you have to know what you're asking for. You can't go in and say, give me the one that has that pyramid. They'll say, which one? Okay. So I took the card and I walked up to the counter and I said to the guy, he said, can I help you? And I said, yeah. And I handed him the card with the GPS locations on it. I said, I'd like to see the four by, there's four by four negatives are about this size. I like to see the four by four mapping negatives for these locations. And on the negative, it's like a hundred by a hundred miles. But they have the tick marks on the outside for GPS. And so he took one look at the card, only to his look at it. And he said, oh, that's the old city on Mars. And he pulled the drawer up and handed me a packet. So I came back with it. I took the negatives, went into town to a photography place. I gave them a copy of the GPSs and the negatives. And I said, load these up the best you can. I want to know if what I described is in these pictures. And everything I described is on those negatives. And they blew them up the best they could so you could see. So that's all in my mind trick book. I was pretty sure that everything I said was correct.

01:27:16.531 --> 01:27:24.631
