01:28:10.411 --> 01:28:33.271
<v SPEAKER_2>Excellent. And thinking on that then, because it's a fascinating case and everything you've said so far, Joe, it sounds like there are very few limitations perhaps on remote viewing. You've said before in interviews that time is largely immaterial. You can go deep into the past. You've remote viewed Mars 1 million BC. But I've heard you talk about future targets being more limited. Is that right?

01:28:33.271 --> 01:34:24.271
<v SPEAKER_1>Oh, yeah. It's much more difficult going into the future. And there's predominantly, there's a reason why. When you go into the past, everything that has occurred or did occur or would occur or might have occurred in the past, we understand the concepts of reality that make itself. In other words, the first flight by the Wright brothers or whoever did the first flight, it happened in the past. We know how planes fly in the air now. So it's not a big surprise to us to know that somebody built a plane and got off the ground first. That's not a big surprise. But where you get a surprise is in going to the future, because the example I use is if in 1975, you were to target a specific room in Slovenia Corporation as a remote viewer, what you would probably get in targeting that room is a glass tube with this sort of cloud-like roiling energy in this tube. And there would be a pencil-thin beam of light, and you would see that light chewing its way through four inches of stainless steel. And on the other end, you would hear a thump, thump, thump, thump. And you could provide all those details. But if somebody said, what is that? You couldn't tell them. You couldn't tell them anything about it. It's like, holy crap, they're using a light beam to chew through metal. How do they do that? I don't know. You know, the conceptualization for a high-energy pump laser doesn't yet exist. So what people would say to the remote viewer is, you're nuts. You're crazy. That's not possible. Okay? So going just a little bit into the future, maybe it's a month. You can be reporting on things in great detail. You have absolutely no understanding for it. And I've done targets like that. And I've done a target like that in the out-of-body condition. And I will tell you, when you do a target like that and you bring it back in such detail, where you can draw an engineering drawing, you know, front view, top view, side view, cut away all the parts, name as many of the parts as you can and what their function is, and you get a feeling for what it might be able to do, it's scary. When you do that and you hand it to somebody in the DOD, and they go, well, wait a minute, stop, don't go any further because it's something they're working on. And you just access it in detail. That's scary. That really makes people worried because I could build that in a garage. You understand? So, yeah, no, they don't want that capability out there. They don't want anybody to have that capability out there. So how do they deal with that? Well, they release everything supposedly on remote viewing. Only they dump all the files on the floor and they shuffle everything together in a big mess. They release it out. And every Tom, Dick and Harry that hears the word remote viewing suddenly goes, oh, that's easy. I just close my eyes and give them a drawing. And before you know it, it's not even valid anymore because the protocol has disappeared into the woodwork. And maybe that's necessary. Maybe that should happen. You know, I'm a firm believer that there's a lot of things wrong with what we do. And because I believe that because we can do something doesn't necessarily mean we should do it. You know, I can tell you, I got as far as I did in remote viewing because I have a solid foundation that I clearly understand my own personal morality and ethics. I will tell you, it's a baseline I'm willing to die for. I don't think many other people know what they will or won't die for. But it's the very reason that I have a clear understanding for what I should or shouldn't be doing, that I can talk about things and there are things I won't do. So, I think there are a lot of people out there that don't have those boundaries. And I think that there's a lot of people out there that do things simply because they can. That's creating a huge problem for humanity. We need to pay more attention to not doing some things just because we can't. It just brings out too many problems. Because the first place somebody wants to go with something when it's got a lot of energy, a lot of power, a lot of capability, it's militarily.

01:34:24.271 --> 01:34:25.331
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah.

01:34:25.331 --> 01:34:52.271
<v SPEAKER_1>We don't need more of that, believe me. I mean, we're getting into an area now where we're capable of fighting proxy wars using machines. And we're introducing AI. That's insanity. That's my personal belief. You can't teach morals and ethics to a machine. It just won't happen.

01:34:52.611 --> 01:34:59.031
<v SPEAKER_2>Do you think, you've mentioned AI, Joe, do you think AI is going to change our relationship with consciousness?

01:35:00.451 --> 01:36:13.671
<v SPEAKER_1>It'll change our relationship with pretty much everything. And in some ways, that's okay. But in many ways, again, we'll be doing things, maybe we shouldn't be. So we're sort of opening the door to a capability we don't know enough about. Control it. And where you can't control things, why do you play in that? You know, a good example, when they decide they're going to find the god particle and they built a CERN accelerator in France and Switzerland. That was a great idea. It was huge. Multi-billion-dollar project, fastest accelerator on the planet. We're going to get to the basis of reality and existence. And what they also theoretically suggested, it might open the door to a black hole that would consume the earth. But they did it anyway. How insane is that? You understand?

01:36:14.011 --> 01:36:27.111
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah. It's that clip from the movie Oppenheimer when they're talking about the chance of it igniting the sky. And he says, almost zero. And they're like, almost zero. And he's like, yeah, almost zero. There's a chance.

01:36:27.111 --> 01:37:17.851
<v SPEAKER_1>What's wrong with these people? They want to go down in history as the eradicator of the human species? I mean, it's... Ah! No morality, no ethics, no... No contemplation for what might happen. But it just... It's crazy. One of the things I do like about remote viewing is you have a capacity for seeing ahead from a perspective that's different any other way. In other words, you can actually go ahead and report on the effects or results of something. You may not know exactly why they're happening, but you will certainly be able to report on them.

