01:45:09.711 --> 01:48:20.071
<v SPEAKER_1>And we vaporized two cities full of people because we could. Now, the excuse is it prevented a lot of deaths continuing to the end of the war. That's bull. They could have stopped the war any other way. They didn't. They wanted to use those weapons. Okay. So what if the next thing is we discover, well, it's obvious that you're not going to go from star to star and meet other entities in a fire wagon. And that includes UFOs. I don't care if you're traveling at five times the speed of light. It's still going to take you 50 years to get the office of jury. So it's a waste of time. I think you get from star to star by folding out of this dimension and folding in to that dimension. You don't even travel through space. You travel through other or whatever that is. Okay. What if we suddenly discover that capability? Should we or shouldn't we? If we decide we should, we have just become a threat to a half million year old advanced civilization because they know we're going to travel star to star in the back room and that ship is going to have a nuke. You know that. So what will happen? We'll get knocked back to sticks and stones. We'll suffer a double massive flare out of the sun or whatever. We suffered a flood that killed most of the people on the planet at one time. Natural disaster is the way we're being controlled. And so if we become a threat, a serious threat, we're going to be back and living in caves and hunting animals again with spears because when you regress, it's all the way down to the flint. It doesn't stop halfway because you only lose 20% of your capability, you can't build a factory anymore. You understand? So I think we need to pay attention to our morals and our ethics as an answer to our introduction to a race or species of entities. It's a half million years ahead of us. That's a wise move. Where wanting to meet and try to get something from them is not a wise move. And so that's just my personal opinion. It could be wrong, but I sure wouldn't want to be thought of as a threat to those entities, whoever they are.

01:48:22.031 --> 01:48:41.331
<v SPEAKER_2>I can understand that. And listen, I want to ask, we're going to get to some listener questions just to finish off. You've answered so many already in the body of the interview. But I want to know, how closely do you follow modern ufology and the UFO topic and what's happened in the last few years with Congress and David Grush and those folks coming forward?

01:48:41.571 --> 01:50:25.431
<v SPEAKER_1>I follow it pretty closely. Ryan Wood and his father, I've known them forever. And in fact, I went to a couple of possible crash sites with Ryan back many years ago. And it was clear that most of them were other things. But there was one site we went to where it was obvious to me had been a UFO crash and somebody had come and gotten it. There were still marks in the desert floor from the big heavy equipment that came and pulled it away. So I know that stuff is out there. And I know they have this stuff and they have probably been working away on it. But I suspect it's going to take another couple hundred years to understand it. But I kind of follow along with it. What I become concerned about is the leaps of judgment that many make. I don't like some of the people in the UFO community that try to out people, because I think they're doing it in a nefarious way, in a bad way. There probably are people in government that know more. But I don't think being hostile towards them and trying to out them in some way to force them to give information up is a wise thing to do. I think in the long run, what they would probably do before giving it up is destroy it. Just my view.

