<v SPEAKER_2>Or is that difficult for you to look at? Because again, it's the verification aspect of it, of what does a flying saucer look like, or what does a craft or a being look like?

02:01:36.511 --> 02:02:42.971
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah, but well, you can get a really good description of the craft. But where the problem comes in is what are they doing with it? There's a cultural difference there. The differences with the Chinese and anybody else, it's completely different because they look at their professors, their researchers differently than we do. If they have snow white hair and they're in their 80s, they make no mistakes, okay? So anything they say is accepted as truth. In America, that would never happen. We'd question anybody for their background, their intelligence, how they were trained, all those sorts of things, if they're a researcher. Chinese don't do that. Everything's perfect for a man over 80 with snow white hair. He can decide what's real and not real, and they'll buy it. So, you know, as good as that.

02:02:42.971 --> 02:02:53.531
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah, yeah, no, no, that's fair. And you were asked about reviewing, remote viewing famous UFO incidents. For example, Roswell, did you ever review anything like that?

02:02:53.531 --> 02:04:25.791
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah, I have done that for three crash sites. One crash site, it turned out, was a bomber. It came down. It was treated just like a UFO collection. They raked the ground. They put everything in sacks, loaded it all on low boys, every piece of wreckage, everything. The reason why it was in 1940, just before our introduction into the Second World War, and it was carrying one of the two prototype Norden bomb sites. That's for bombing from high altitude at night. It made it more accurate. They did not want one piece of that bomb site getting in anybody's hands. It was all identical to how you would collect a UFO. That's the one that sticks out in my mind the most. But there were two others. One, we never were able to confirm or deny anything about it. But another was one we felt was truly a UFO site. What was interesting about that is there was some material collected from it, and we actually went back to the place where they stored it, and were able to access the safe. The logbook was there where it logged in, but it was no longer in the safe. So we were unable to obtain it.

02:04:28.171 --> 02:04:33.171
<v SPEAKER_2>Have you ever encountered alternate timelines or dimensions in your work?

02:04:33.171 --> 02:06:22.531
<v SPEAKER_1>I don't believe in them. I already believe that every human being on this planet is in a world of their own. The reason I say that is you're always with different people, and when you go and do something where you're alone, and you come back and you're with people you know, and they say, what did you do yesterday? You can tell them, but they can never know that for sure. They have to take your word for it, which means you're in a different bubble of the world than they are. So our worlds are all different, and how we express them comes out in our interaction with one another. It's why it's important that we don't shut down when we meet somebody that's new or somebody different. They have information that could be of value to us always. So you want to make friends with everybody you meet. Even people you don't like, you can learn from. You learn from your enemies. Some of it's not good. You may be making assumptions that are wrong, and that's why they remain your enemy. Where in fact, you may find common ground, and it makes the enemy idea go away. You know, it's just, I've always felt every human being is in a different world than mine, and I can learn something from them. And so I've always wanted to be as open as possible with people. I don't want to deny anybody, or condemn them automatically. I mean, there are bad people in the world. I've met evil people, and I won't have anything to do with them. But I have to meet them and really come to know that first.

02:06:24.251 --> 02:06:32.811
<v SPEAKER_2>In your near-death experience, you described distressed souls in a tunnel. What did they look like and what did that experience mean to you?

02:06:35.191 --> 02:11:21.071
<v SPEAKER_1>When I had that experience, I was DOA for 38 minutes. I had no breathing and no heartbeat. Now, I don't believe that. I think maybe I had a heartbeat every 6 minutes. Maybe I took a shallow breath every now and then. But that's immaterial. I was out of body the whole time watching the entire event. So when I eventually was in the emergency room, watching them cutting the clothing off me, I felt heat on the back of my neck, and I thought it was one of those bright lights in the ceiling. So I turned around to see if I was close to one, and I was enveloped in a more intense light, white light. It was 10 times more intense, but it didn't bother me, because I was, I no longer had my eyes. I figured I was just in a pure form there. I know my body wasn't there. I was just one with this light. And my perception was that it was God. I got in an argument with the voice and the light, because the voice said, you can't be here. You have to go back. And I said, no, no, no, no, no, no. I don't want to go anywhere, because it was so comfortable. And then I woke up, I sat up, and I was covered with a sheet. I had tubes all in me. So I kept telling people, God's a white light. You can't cease to exist. You come with you. You are gifted with all the knowledge of all the effects you've had on everybody you've ever interacted with. And then some. And it sort of paints you with a brush and colors that says, this is what I am. That's heaven and hell, by the way. You can either be painted very, very dark, or you can be painted very, very bright. And that depicts how you're received by others in that state. But I started telling people that. In the military, they put me in a rest home for two weeks, because they thought I had serious brain damage. So I wouldn't stop talking about it. And the third psychiatrist I was with said, you have to act normal, you're not going to leave here. So I started acting normal. I got reassigned to the different unit. But they let everybody think I was dead. Anyway, I matured a lot. But for six months afterwards, I hated being here, because this is so primitive. I thought of about 30 ways of taking my own life. I was seriously contemplating that. And one night I got pulled back out of body, back into that light. And some, and I don't think it's God now, but it was a, it may have been a reflection of me. I don't know. But I got speed slapped and told, look, if you know what's going to happen when you die, why don't you pay more attention to being here? Do something right. And so I've been trying to do what I can here. And what's interesting about that is I've had like three or four near death experiences where I've come so close. And right at the last second, it gets reversed. And it's like, you still aren't done, go back. You still aren't done, go back. And I've had MRSA twice in the hospital after surgeries. The second time I had 108 temperature, 107 temperature, I almost died. They fought 33 hours to keep me alive. I was ready to go. I didn't go. It's like, every time I think it's going to happen, I'm sent back. So, I don't know if I'm doing something right or I'm doing something wrong. Depends on your viewpoint on where you want to be. But, you know, I do know that everybody has an opportunity to do good, and good's relative. Good for how you view it. You got to do what you perceive is the right thing, and not do something just simply because you want it. What's interesting is, I lack for nothing. I have anything I want when I need it, and my wife is cared for and I'm comfortable, and I don't need a lot. I'm enjoying this place now.

02:11:22.671 --> 02:11:34.451
<v SPEAKER_2>A lot of folks asked you once from, you've remote viewed your own death. And a lot of folks were asking, why did you do that? And has it changed the way you live?

02:11:34.451 --> 02:13:50.111
<v SPEAKER_1>It's changed it greatly. I don't think it's possible to remote view death itself. There's a gate there. You can't get past it. I used to make fun of mediums. I used to walk in the office sometimes and pick a stapler up and go, oh, oh, I'm getting a message from the dead. The guy who made this stapler, I made fun of mediums because I didn't believe in it. And then I was working a problem where an undercover agent had disappeared and they didn't know why. And I had discovered that he drove off the road and wrecked his car and died. And they said, how do you know he's dead? And I said, because he's floating horizontally in front of me with his hands crossed on his chest and his eyes are closed and he's drifting away. And they said, the monitor said, oh, this is where we can find out about death, right? So he says, following. And I said, okay, and I'm trying to follow him. I said, I can't get closer to him. He keeps getting further away. And he says, follow him anyway. You're a remote viewer, you can do anything. And I said, I can't get, and he kept yelling at me. And I said, look, there's these gauze like things coming down, it's making me more difficult to even see him. I can't get through them. And he said, well, do it anyway. And I said, look, I can't go beyond the veils. And as soon as I said it, I went, I just got taped being a medium. So I changed my whole attitude. I went to a place in New York where they have mediums, and I spent three or four days there with them. And then I went down to Florida to where they have them down in Florida. And I spent two or three days on there, and I met these mediums, and they're truly good people. They're honest people. They're actually helping. Now some of them aren't as gifted as others, like some remote viewers aren't as gifted as other remote viewers. But they're trying. And so I don't badmouth mediums anymore. I know exactly what they're doing. And I think they're doing a good work. So do we know about that?

02:13:50.111 --> 02:13:50.911
<v SPEAKER_2>Final question.

02:13:50.911 --> 02:13:52.591
<v SPEAKER_1>No.

02:13:52.591 --> 02:14:08.471
<v SPEAKER_2>Final question, Joe, because you've been with me in such a long time, and you've been so good in this interview. I really appreciate it. And I'm sure folks have loved it. Looking back over your life and your career now, what do we still most misunderstand about consciousness or whatever that might be?

02:14:08.471 --> 02:17:46.511
<v SPEAKER_1>About consciousness? You have to remember that you got to remember this, OK? Everything we think is real isn't. Our entire consciousness, even the part that's in our head, is locked inside a bone box. The only way we know anything is through the sensing devices that we have. Ears, the smeller, the eyes, skin. There's all kinds of sensing devices we have for interpreting our environment. What we are actually walking around in is an energy field, a massive energy field filled with all kinds of different energies. And they all represent something. And these sensors tell us that we are actually in a world. Okay? But that world is our creation. No two people on the planet create the same world. It's a mistake to make the assumption that we all are talking about the same thing. We are not. We are talking about our personal perceptions. So, to label people because they don't think like you is wrong. To label somebody because they do think like you is wrong. We can learn from everybody. And we shouldn't condemn anyone because we don't like how they perceive or how they present. There's a reason for that. We need to know the reason. We don't need to condemn them. And so, you know, people are flawed. We're all flawed because we have to deal with reality through a sensing system that's dysfunctional. The one thing we have an advantage on is that all of our mind is not here. All of our mind is. It's the reason why this whole energy field exists. I think we have an agreement that it's all here because it's a place we can come and experience and do things that teach us something about who and what we are. And in terms of knowing God, I think God is just as blind as we are. I think God is a neutral creation that has no understanding for what it is. And we are like the fingers. We're the examples or the experiment that goes out and discovers and learns and understands what good and evil is to help God understand what God is. In other words, we're all part of the creator. And if we're all part of the creator, then we have a potential to be extraordinarily good and not evil. So I just prefer to be extraordinarily as good as I can be. Doesn't mean I don't make mistakes. I really do. I screw up big time sometimes. I'd pay for that. But, you know, I've just become a lot more open, I think, understanding that I'm different like everybody else is.

